---
import DocLayout from "../../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import { routeFor } from "../../i18n";
---

<DocLayout
  lang="en"
  page="docs/ai"
  title="Search, AI, and publishing"
  intro="Opaline starts with reliable local search. Semantic retrieval, AI assistance, and publishing should stay explainable and permissioned."
  next={[
    { href: routeFor("en", "privacy"), label: "Privacy" },
    { href: routeFor("en", "docs/release"), label: "Build and updates" },
  ]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>Search comes first</h2>
    <p>Keyword search is the first reliable layer because it works locally, is easy to explain, and maps directly to the HTML documents in the workspace.</p>
    <p>Semantic search and AI retrieval are later layers. They should improve discovery without hiding which notes, headings, or blocks were used.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Visible sources</h2>
    <p>AI answers should cite the workspace material they relied on. When a response references a note, the user should be able to open that source and decide whether the answer is trustworthy.</p>
    <div class="doc-note">
      <p>Private notes should not be sent to a cloud model without clear user intent, provider configuration, and visible scope.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>AI editing protocol</h2>
    <p>AI-assisted edits should be commands, not hidden rewrites. The current development build routes editor actions through a unified command registry shared by toolbar actions, context menus, slash commands, the command palette, and future AI editing flows.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Commands expose localized labels, descriptions, categories, shortcuts, parameter schemas, availability checks, and UI visibility.</li>
      <li>AI-visible metadata records permissions, whether a command mutates the note, whether confirmation is required, whether a history snapshot is recommended, and risk level.</li>
      <li>AI operation plans can be previewed to identify unknown, unavailable, experimental, or high-risk commands.</li>
      <li>Execution validates parameters, and failed execution can restore the editor HTML from before the operation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is still infrastructure for safer editing. A real AI writing experience also needs readable change previews, visible sources, and user confirmation.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Publishing</h2>
    <p>Publishing should be a deliberate export path from local HTML documents. It should not change the workspace source of truth or silently expose private material.</p>
  </section>
</DocLayout>
